[node:f0e2ae72-268a-4859-80b0-e98ec768fb10]
type: constraint
tier: mid-term
path: /artifact/a-layout-is-part-of-the-shell-not-upstr
created_at: 2026-09-08T19:56:57.176951+00:00
needs_review: false
retrieval_weight: 1.0
trust_weight: 1.0
archived: false
-> ABOUT [node:49ba726c-031e-4a01-839f-11a76a8bf009] @ 2026-09-08T19:56:57.176951+00:00
-> DEPENDS_ON [node:8f453152-4703-4c6e-8a7c-4ae5ca69b54e] @ 2026-09-08T19:57:08.915658+00:00
-> HAS_FACET [node:a1174884-8d33-4260-af5b-0f97264a2ea2] @ 2026-09-08T19:56:57.176951+00:00
-> HAS_FACET [node:e12ca220-5f82-4226-a2eb-9964701f03c4] @ 2026-09-08T19:56:57.176951+00:00
-> ABOUT [node:f96f62c3-d72a-4c40-8e16-57278ca1f302] @ 2026-09-08T19:56:57.176951+00:00

A Layout is part of the shell, not upstream data, and caching it on the device is what makes an outage screen honest. The service worker deliberately does not cache the API, because the backend owns data freshness and a second cache in front of it would answer with a lie - but applying that rule to the layout produced a reload during an outage that painted an empty deck, so the reconnect scrim covered a void rather than the last screen the household saw. The shape of the screen is kept locally and repainted before the scrim goes over it; the data inside the cards is still not, and the footer says the layout is the last known one rather than pretending it is current.
---
[node:b347fe70-0841-4c01-95c2-6019fa19423f]
type: constraint
tier: mid-term
path: /artifact/headless-chrome-s-virtual-time-budget-do
created_at: 2026-09-08T19:56:57.300588+00:00
needs_review: false
retrieval_weight: 1.0
trust_weight: 1.0
archived: false
-> HAS_FACET [node:a1174884-8d33-4260-af5b-0f97264a2ea2] @ 2026-09-08T19:56:57.300588+00:00
-> HAS_FACET [node:c62c070b-e4a6-41b3-86f3-00ad5d96800f] @ 2026-09-08T19:56:57.300588+00:00

Headless Chrome's virtual-time budget does not drive service worker installation, so a service worker cannot be verified with the screenshot-and-dump-DOM approach that works for ordinary pages. A test that way reports a blank error page and looks exactly like a broken worker; the worker in question was in fact registering, activating and caching correctly. Verification needs a real browser driven over the devtools protocol with real waits - controller present, registration activated, cache populated - and an offline check needs the backend actually stopped between a warm load and a reload, not a network condition simulated in the page.
